        ELSE JUST FALSE

-- "specified contractual dispute": relating to a payment of money, arising out of or in the course of employment under a contract of service, about a matter in the First Schedule.
-- src:263 | “specified contractual dispute” means a dispute, relating to a
-- src:264 | payment of an amount of money, which arises out of or in the
-- src:265 | course of employment under a contract of service, about any
-- src:266 | matter specified in the First Schedule;
@ref s 2(1) “specified contractual dispute” (src:263-266)
GIVEN dispute IS A Dispute
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(1) the dispute is a specified contractual dispute` MEANS
    IF dispute's `relates to a payment of an amount of money` AND dispute's `arises out of or in the course of employment under a contract of service`
    THEN CONSIDER dispute's matter
         WHEN `A matter in the First Schedule` item THEN JUST TRUE
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“specified contractual dispute” means a dispute, relating to a payment of an amount of money, which arises out of or in the course of employment under a contract of service, about any matter specified in the First Schedule"
         WHEN `A matter in the Second Schedule` item THEN JUST FALSE
         WHEN `A matter in the Third Schedule` item THEN JUST FALSE
         WHEN `None of the matters in the Schedules` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“specified contractual dispute” means a dispute, relating to a payment of an amount of money, which arises out of or in the course of employment under a contract of service"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- "specified statutory dispute": relating to a payment of money, about a matter in the Second Schedule.
-- src:272 | “specified statutory dispute” means a dispute, relating to a
-- src:273 | payment of an amount of money, about any matter specified
-- src:274 | in the Second Schedule;
@ref s 2(1) “specified statutory dispute” (src:272-274)
GIVEN dispute IS A Dispute
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(1) the dispute is a specified statutory dispute` MEANS
    IF dispute's `relates to a payment of an amount of money`
    THEN CONSIDER dispute's matter
         WHEN `A matter in the Second Schedule` item THEN JUST TRUE
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“specified statutory dispute” means a dispute, relating to a payment of an amount of money, about any matter specified in the Second Schedule"
         WHEN `A matter in the First Schedule` item THEN JUST FALSE
         WHEN `A matter in the Third Schedule` item THEN JUST FALSE
         WHEN `None of the matters in the Schedules` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“specified statutory dispute” means a dispute, relating to a payment of an amount of money"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- "wrongful dismissal dispute": relating to the dismissal of an employee, specified in the Third Schedule.
