§§ `s 2(2) and (3) -- counterclaims, and the Commissioner's officers`

-- s 2(2): a rule of reading.  When the Act is applied to a counterclaim, a reference to a claimant is a reference to the person making the counterclaim, and a reference to a respondent is a reference to the person against whom it is made.
-- src:302 | (2) When this Act is applied to a counterclaim lodged with a
-- src:303 | tribunal —
-- src:308 | (a) any reference to a claimant is a reference to a person
-- src:309 | making the counterclaim; and
-- src:311 | (b) any reference to a respondent is a reference to a person
-- src:312 | against whom the counterclaim is made.
@ref s 2(2) (src:302-312)
GIVEN role IS A `Role named in the Act`
      `the Act is applied to a counterclaim lodged with a tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person making the counterclaim` IS A Person
      `the person against whom the counterclaim is made` IS A Person
GIVETH A MAYBE Person
`s 2(2) the person a reference in the Act is a reference to` MEANS
    IF `the Act is applied to a counterclaim lodged with a tribunal`
    THEN CONSIDER role
         WHEN `The claimant` THEN JUST `the person making the counterclaim`
         WHEN `The respondent` THEN JUST `the person against whom the counterclaim is made`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording) [no yes-or-no]: s 2(2) speaks only of the case "When this Act is applied to a counterclaim" and the function returns a person, so there is no JUST FALSE to give; for an ordinary claim the words keep their ordinary meaning under s 2(1)
    ELSE NOTHING

